<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Protestantism</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Protestantism is the branch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> that arose from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> of the sixteenth century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a> and others broke with the Roman Catholic Church. Its main traditions, Lutheran, Reformed and Anglican, rejected papal authority and stressed the authority of scripture and salvation by faith. It became the established religion of much of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and spread worldwide through migration and missions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/protestantism.jpg" alt="Protestantism" width="320" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Torsten Schleese · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lutherbibel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
